"><span>Terrifying and unpredictable destruction</span></h2><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Ziha Yit=
ie, a tour guide, brought a group of hikers to a high-altitude observation =
deck in Tibet on Wednesday morning for a view of the snowcapped mountains o=
ver the border in Nepal.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>As they took in the dramatic view=
s, my colleagues report, the group began hearing a series of =E2=80=9Cdeep =
rumbles.=E2=80=9D Eventually they heard a loud crack that =E2=80=9Csounded =
like explosives detonating inside the mountains,=E2=80=9D as Ziha put it, f=
ollowed by a wall of noise, then a cloud of dust.</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>What the=
y were witnessing, though they didn=E2=80=99t know it at the time, was the =
beginning of a disaster that would, at latest count, kill at least 700 and =
leave more than 3,000 missing. A glacier was collapsing. Scientists are sti=
ll working out the precise chain of events, but what started out as plummet=
ing ice in the high Himalayas would go on to cut a path of destruction at l=
east 80 kilometers long.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>My colleague Anupreeta Das has be=
en in Nepal covering the aftermath of the disaster. When I spoke to her, sh=
e painted a harrowing picture =E2=80=94 of villages so thoroughly swept awa=
y that they were barely recognizable as former villages from the air; of la=
rge trucks tossed by the water and deposited at odd angles, as if by a care=
